3. Solution A - Capacity Planning
3.1 Domain Architecture
Prometheus metrics + Splunk logs/errors + ServiceNow incidents + topology + OCP capacity metadata feed a Capacity Context Builder, which drives a Capacity Playbook Set and produces capacity findings and recommendations.

Component

Purpose

Context Builder Inputs

Prometheus metrics, Splunk logs/errors, ServiceNow incidents, topology context, and OCP capacity metadata.

Capacity Playbook Set

Baseline utilization; under-utilization; over-utilization; dependency-induced stress; incident-aware capacity risk.

Primary Output

Capacity findings and recommendations.

 

3.2 Capacity Workflow (End-to-End)
Step 1: Collect 90-day windows

• CPU usage (%), requests/limits, throttling, memory (supporting), and restarts.
• Request volume, latency p95/p99, and error rates.
• Splunk exceptions, timeout patterns, and retry signatures.
• Incident counts and severity.
Step 2: Build baseline

• For each app/service/environment, compute CPU average (daily and weekly), CPU p95/p99, peak-to-median ratio, and day-of-week / hour-of-day seasonality.
Step 3: Classify utilization

• Under-utilized candidate: sustained low average CPU, low p99, and low error/incident risk.
• Over-utilized candidate: high average or high p99 plus throttling, latency, or error signals.
• Unstable candidate: wide volatility, incident spikes, and dependency risk markers.
Step 4: Dependency-aware diagnosis

• Trace upstream burst traffic and retry amplification.
• Trace downstream latency and DB bottlenecks.
• Determine whether stress origin is local or propagated.
Step 5: Produce recommendation package

• Right-size requests/limits.
• Adjust HPA/VPA policies.
• Tune retry and timeout behavior.
• Recommend service-chain remediation actions where dependency effects are observed.
Step 6: Track outcomes

• Compare pre- and post-recommendation KPIs for closed-loop learning.
3.3 Capacity Decision Rules (Initial)
Source note

The notes state that the current rules use low data for development and can be customized later.
 

Rule

Logic

Rule CP-UNDER-01 (Under-utilized)

Conditions for 4+ weeks: CPU average < 25%; CPU p99 < 45%; no sustained incident-linked performance impact. Action: recommend reducing CPU request/limit by a safe step (10-20%).

Rule CP-OVER-01 (Over-utilized)

Conditions for 2+ weeks: CPU average > 70% OR CPU p99 > 90%, plus at least one of throttling threshold breach, latency p99 breach, or error spike. Action: recommend scaling and bottleneck analysis.

Rule CP-DEP-01 (Dependency-induced)

Conditions: local CPU/latency spikes correlate with downstream latency/error spikes; upstream request burst or retry amplification is observed. Action: flag local scaling-only action as insufficient and recommend chain-level fixes.

 

