4. Solution B - Resiliency Test Engineering (RTAAS)
Important note from source

The team needs to understand exactly which capabilities are available through Net Havoc APIs.
The solution also depends on trustworthy upstream/downstream path discovery, not only code scanning.
 

4.1 Domain Architecture
Git static scan + runtime telemetry + Net Havoc capabilities + topology critical paths + OCP allocation/config feed a Resilience Context Builder, which drives a Resiliency Playbook Set and produces resilience findings, a gap backlog, and scorecards.

Component

Purpose

Context Builder Inputs

Git static scan, runtime telemetry, Net Havoc capabilities, topology critical paths, OCP allocation/config.

Resiliency Playbook Set

Capability discovery; sufficiency assessment; test selection; Net Havoc execution; recovery validation.

Primary Outputs

Resilience findings, gap backlog, and scorecards.

 

4.2 RTAAS Workflow (End-to-End)
Step 1: Discover implemented capabilities

• Static scan from Git/config for patterns such as timeout, retry, circuit breaker, fallback, bulkhead, rate limit, and idempotency.
Step 2: Map capabilities to components and critical paths

• For each service and key dependency path, identify which patterns are present.
• Identify which paths are still unprotected.
Step 3: Determine what is needed vs allocated

• Base the assessment on criticality, SLOs, failure modes, and traffic profile.
• Check whether configured resilience is sufficient.
• Check whether OCP resource requests/limits align to resilience objectives.
Step 4: Select playbooks and run tests in Net Havoc

• Fault scenarios by dependency type include API latency injection, API error injection, DB slowdown / unavailable, queue lag / backpressure, and pod restart / kill chaos events where allowed.
Step 5: Validate behavior and classify outcome

• Did timeout trigger correctly?
• Did retry amplify failure?
• Did circuit breaker protect upstreams?
• Was fallback graceful?
• Did service recover within target time?
Step 6: Publish actionable outputs

• Gap list with severity and ownership.
• Test evidence with pass/fail and recovery timelines.
• Prioritized remediation backlog.
4.3 Resilience Decision Rules (Initial)
Rule

Logic

Rule RE-DISC-01 (Capability missing on critical path)

If a critical dependency path lacks timeout, circuit breaker, or fallback protections, mark it as a high-risk gap.

Rule RE-SUFF-01 (Capability present but insufficient)

If a pattern exists but thresholds or configuration do not meet SLO failure budgets, mark as medium/high risk depending on blast radius.

Rule RE-VAL-01 (Dynamic validation failure)

If a Net Havoc scenario fails the recovery objective, create a remediation recommendation and a mandatory retest.

Resource alignment rule from notes

If OCP requests/limits do not support graceful degradation under load/fault, recommend reallocation and autoscaling policy tuning.

 

4.4 Required Data Points (Resiliency Engineering)
Category

Details

Service criticality and objectives

service_tier, business criticality, SLOs (availability/latency), error budget, RTO/RPO where applicable.

Capability inventory (static)

has_timeout with timeout_ms; has_retry with retry_count and backoff_strategy; has_circuit_breaker with open_threshold and half_open_policy; has_fallback with fallback_type; has_bulkhead with isolation boundaries; has_rate_limiter with policy; idempotency support.

